{"ts":"2026-08-18T15:24:48.048Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Good. Now I have full understanding. My deliverable: a mitigation catalogue in `mitigation/` binding safeguards to the hazards from the L06 trade-off analysis of the selected approach (SQLite-backed DurableScarStore). Since there's no separate L06 artifact file, the trade-off analysis is embodied in the RB-0026 dossier + the two intakes (which record the candidate approaches: e.g., in-module DurableScarStore vs separate module sqlite_store.py, SQLite vs other stores, etc.). I should emit:\n\n1. `mitigation/MITIGATION-CATALOGUE-RB-0026.md` — the main catalogue: hazards (with severity), safeguards (mechanism: KILL_SWITCH / FALLBACK / SHADOW_MODE / SAFETY_CONSTRAINT), accepted risks, plus the gate check that every HIGH hazard is covered.\n2. Maybe a machine-readable JSON version mirroring the MitigationCatalogue dataclasses, so the PROOF evidence can be mechanically checked (has_high_severity_coverage semantics).\n\nThe hazards to catalogue (trade-off analysis of the selected approach — durable SQLite scar store alongside in-memory store, wired into ops/autonomous_run.py):\n\nH1 (HIGH): Durable scar poisoning across runs — a bad/over-broad scar persisted once would poison every future run forever (previously the process exit was an implicit reset). Safeguard: T3 quarantine rule enforced in the durable store too (ScarQuarantined for < MIN_SIGNATURE_TAGS), plus subset-match containment preserved exactly, plus the store lives at state_dir/scars.db which is deletable (kill switch: delete file). Mechanism: SAFETY_CONSTRAINT + KILL_SWITCH.\n\nH2 (HIGH): Contract drift between in-memory ScarStore and DurableScarStore (match semantics, ID sequence stability, injected_scars gating INJECTION_STAGES) causing L18 injection to behave differently across processes. Safeguard: shared logic reuse + parity tests (test_scar_durability.py proving (1) cross-instance persistence, (2) quarantine, (3) INJECTION_STAGES gating), plus regression run of test_scars.py/test_evolution.py (SAFETY_CONSTRAINT / SHADOW_MODE-ish). Mechanism: SAFETY_CONSTRAINT.\n\nH3 (HIGH): ID stability/collision across reopen — scar IDs must stay stable across reopen (scar-NNNN sequence derived from max existing id); a re-derived counter colliding or resetting would orphan or shadow precedents referenced by ledger/CAS. Safeguard: derive counter from MAX(id) on load; IDs immutable; test asserts stability. Mechanism: SAFETY_CONSTRAINT.\n\nH4 (MEDIUM): File I/O failure/corruption of scars.db (disk full, concurrent access, SQLite lock contention under ConcurrentScheduler's parallel execution) — accepted risk mitigated by SQLite WAL/locking, single-writer serialization via evolution_lock already present; failure mode degrades to... actually a raise would crash autonomous_run. Safeguard: sqlite3 stdlib handles concurrency; state_dir colocated with ledger.db which already works under same pattern. Mechanism: FALLBACK (accepted risk with fallback: store colocated with ledger which already proves the pattern).\n\nH5 (MEDIUM): Latency/memory overhead — SQLite connection per store instance; match_scars loads rows. Scar counts are small (institutional lessons, not telemetry), O(n) subset match same as in-memory. Accepted risk.\n\nH6 (MEDIUM): No schema migration/versioning for scars.db — accepted risk (single-row schema, additive changes; reversible: delete db → back to in-memory behavior of zero scars).\n\nH7 (LOW): Unbounded growth of the scar corpus over long-lived operations — scars grow monotonically; matched injections grow prompt size. Accepted risk (FR-6 keeps scars gated by subset match; future compaction is a separate intake). \n\nH8 (LOW/MEDIUM): Secrets/PII? Not really relevant.\n\nH9: Backward compatibility — do NOT break existing in-memory ScarStore or its tests. Safeguard: add alongside, reuse shared logic; regression tests test_scars.py & test_evolution.py must still pass. Mechanism: SAFETY_CONSTRAINT.\n\nAlso a kill-switch / rollback: reverting ops/autonomous_run.py wiring returns to in-memory behavior; deleting scars.db resets memory (also a recovery lever). Reversibility per intake: \"reversible: true\".\n\nAlso homogeneous-audit constraint (GLM-5.3-only window) — that's an execution constraint; could be an accepted risk noting seals from this window carry caveat. Actually that's about seals, not this change per se. Could include as accepted risk AR: implementation occurs during single-model interim window; mitigation: deterministic kernel guarantees remain in force + re-run proof under hybrid when quota returns (per RB doc). That's a nice binding to context.\n\nFormat: I'll write both:\n- mitigation/mitigation-catalogue-rb0026.md (human-readable PROOF artifact)\n- mitigation/mitigation-catalogue-rb0026.json (machine-readable, matching runtime.mitigation dataclass shape: hazards[{id,title,severity}], safeguards[{id,hazard_id,mechanism,description}], accepted_risks[{id,hazard_id,rationale,approver}]) — so it can be loaded into MitigationCatalogue and has_high_severity_coverage() verified.\n\nAnd maybe a verification script? I could verify the JSON against the runtime.mitigation module by running python. That would produce live PROOF. Let me do that: write the files, then run a python check that loads the JSON into MitigationCatalogue and asserts has_high_severity_coverage() is True and every hazard is covered. That's my validation.\n\nMechanisms enum: KILL_SWITCH, FALLBACK, SHADOW_MODE, SAFETY_CONSTRAINT.\n\nLet me design the catalogue carefully, binding to the objective's specifics:\n\nHazards (from the L06 trade-off axes for the selected approach — \"SQLite-backed DurableScarStore alongside in-memory ScarStore, stdlib-only, wired in build_scheduler\"):\n\n- HZ-01 HIGH — Durable scar poisoning: persistence turns a previously self-limiting defect (bad scar dies at process exit) into a permanent cross-run poison vector. Safeguards:\n  - SG-01 SAFETY_CONSTRAINT: T3 quarantine preserved verbatim in DurableScarStore.propose_scar — signatures with < MIN_SIGNATURE_TAGS raise ScarQuarantined before any write (write-through only after the gate).\n  - SG-02 SAFETY_CONSTRAINT: match semantics frozen to subset containment exactly as ScarStore (scar.signature <= goal_tags), so a scar can only fire on goals meeting ALL its conditions.\n  - SG-03 KILL_SWITCH: scars.db is a plain SQLite file at state_dir/scars.db; deleting it (or reverting the ops/autonomous_run.py wiring) returns the system to zero-scar in-memory behavior — full memory reset is a single reversible operation.\n\n- HZ-02 HIGH — Contract drift between the durable variant and the in-memory ScarStore breaks L18 injection assumptions (injected_scars gated by INJECTION_STAGES; match_scars ordering by id; get_precedent). Safeguard:\n  - SG-04 SAFETY_CONSTRAINT: same public contract (propose_scar, match_scars, get_precedent, injected_scars) with shared matching logic reused from the module; tests/runtime/test_scar_durability.py proves parity on the three required properties (cross-instance persistence, quarantine, INJECTION_STAGES gating), and test_scars.py + test_evolution.py must still pass (acceptance criterion 4 of the intake) — the existing in-memory store and its tests are untouched.\n\n- HZ-03 HIGH — Scar ID instability across reopen (counter resets → duplicate IDs → get_precedent ambiguity, _scar_refs CAS content collisions shadowing older lessons). Safeguard:\n  - SG-05 SAFETY_CONSTRAINT: counter derived at construction from max existing row id (scar-NNNN sequence continues); IDs immutable once written; durability test asserts the same scar id is returned on a fresh instance.\n\n- HZ-04 MEDIUM — SQLite contention/corruption under ConcurrentScheduler parallel runs (two processes writing scars.db). Accepted risk + partial safeguard:\n  - SG-06 FALLBACK: colocate with ledger.db in state_dir (the ledger already runs SQLite under the same concurrency with WAL); single-committer L3 authoring is already serialized by evolution_lock (IP-0005) inside a process; sqlite3's own locking arbitrates cross-process. Failure surface = same as the existing ledger, not new. Actually maybe better: safeguard mechanism FALLBACK: if the durable store cannot be opened, build_scheduler falls back to the in-memory ScarStore? Hmm — the objective doesn't specify a fallback. Proposing a fallback is a design decision for L08/L11. I can propose it as the bound safeguard since mitigation stage designs safeguards: \"build_scheduler constructs durable store; on construction failure, fall back to in-memory ScarStore with a logged warning\" — that's a FALLBACK safeguard that L08/L11 will carry into the design. That's exactly what mitigation stage is for. I'll include it, marked as binding requirement for downstream stages.\n  - AR-xx accepted risk: transient SQLITE_BUSY under extreme parallelism — bounded by existing single-writer serialization and tiny write volume (one row per run).\n\n- HZ-05 MEDIUM — Latency/memory: durable store adds file I/O on construction (load all rows) and on each propose. Accepted risk: scar corpus is small (institutional lessons, ~O(runs)); O(n) match identical to in-memory; SQLite is stdlib, no new dependency. AR with rationale.\n\n- HZ-06 LOW — Schema evolution: no migration path for scars.db. Accepted risk: additive/one-table schema; deleting the file is an accepted (documented) reset because durability only began existing with this change — there is no legacy data to lose. AR.\n\n- HZ-07 LOW — Unbounded scar growth over long-lived operation inflates matched injections. Accepted risk: subset-match gating (FR-6) means only relevant scars inject; compaction/decay is deferred to a future intake (L18 scar lifecycle). AR.\n\n- HZ-08 MEDIUM — Implementation executes during the GLM-5.3-only interim window (homogeneous audit caveat). Accepted risk per RB-0026: deterministic kernel guarantees (court reproduction, evidence binding, taint index, junk-artifact filter, model attribution) remain in force; re-run proof intake under hybrid when GPT quota returns (~2026-08-22) and record delta. Approver: Founder-directed audit protocol (notebook Entry 142).\n\nHmm, should I include HZ-08? It's an execution-environment risk flagged in the RB doc. Yes, as accepted risk — it's documented intent.\n\nAlso hazard: \"do not break existing in-memory ScarStore\" — covered in HZ-02.\n\nEvery HIGH hazard covered by safeguard: HZ-01 (SG-01,02,03), HZ-02 (SG-04), HZ-03 (SG-05). Good.\n\nAlso include the trade-off matrix reference (L06 axes) — mapping each hazard to trade-off axis: risk (poisoning), accuracy (contract parity), maintainability/complexity (stdlib-only, no new deps), latency/memory (HZ-05), risk/ops (HZ-04, 08).\n\nNow, also note in the catalogue how each safeguard becomes a binding constraint for L08 DESIGN / L11 PLAN: e.g., acceptance criteria for the implementation (the intake's 4 accept lines) — that's the handoff.\n\nFile names: mitigation/ doesn't exist yet. I'll create:\n- mitigation/MITIGATION-CATALOGUE-RB-0026.md\n- mitigation/mitigation_catalogue_rb0026.json\n\nThen verify: run python to load JSON into runtime.mitigation dataclasses and check has_high_severity_coverage() and full coverage. That's my PROOF validation.\n\nLet me also double check the kernel StageId / lifecycle for naming conventions, and check whether \"TRADEOFF\" stage exists in StageId (kernel/types.py has TRADEOFF? From patch_chat_bridge.py it added IDEATE, RESEARCH... test_lifecycle _RATIFIED_CHAIN: \"DESIGN, CRITIQUE, PLAN, ...\" — TRADEOFF appears in lifecycle edges TRADEOFF->MITIGATION). Fine.\n\nAlso should mention mitigation/ artifact naming — check if there's an existing convention. There's no mitigation/ dir. Other stage dirs: design/, plan/, ideation/? Let me check if design/ has artifacts from earlier stages of other goals — design/ contains only static docs. OK, I'm free to name.\n\nOne more consideration: the \"Required evidence (each MUST be produced for the gate to pass): mitigations (PROOF): catalogue binding safeguards to hazards emitted\". So the catalogue must clearly \"emit\" hazards and bind safeguards. The JSON + MD both do this. Also I should include a self-verification command/output in the MD (proof that has_high_severity_coverage holds).\n\nLet me write the files now. First create the directory via editor tool (file creation creates parent dirs? The editor tool \"creates the file\" — likely creates parents; if not I'll mkdir). I'll write the JSON first, then MD, then run verification.\n\nJSON structure mirroring MitigationCatalogue:\n\n{\n  \"goal\": \"RB-0026 durable institutional memory (DurableScarStore)\",\n  \"goal_id\": \"g-70b70a4a60ae\",\n  \"stage\": \"MITIGATION\",\n  \"role\": \"PLANNER\",\n  \"hazards\": [...],\n  \"safeguards\": [...],\n  \"accepted_risks\": [...]\n}\n\nNow write. Also include in MD: upstream refs (L06 trade-off basis: RB-0026 dossier, PAEOS_DURABLE_MEMORY_RB_v0.1.md, ops/backlog_rb0026_durable_scars.json intake), trade-off axes table, hazard catalogue, safeguards, accepted risks, coverage proof, downstream bindings for L08/L11.\n\nThen verify with python: construct MitigationCatalogue from JSON, assert has_high_severity_coverage(), assert every hazard id covered, assert mechanism values valid. Use the repo's venv: check .venv exists? They use `uv run pytest`. Let me check python availability. I'll run `uv run python` or `.venv/bin/python`. Let me check."}}
